[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Basics - read this first
UID: DOC-GUIDE
VERSION: 1.0

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: basic.sgra

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**This document exists to let a beginner and an AI use `.sdoc` right away.**

This set lays out the smallest thing that still holds together as a requirements
specification. System requirements, software requirements, test cases and review
findings **sit in separate files, and the links run between them.** Start your own
specification by **copying this whole set and replacing the content.**

**This paragraph carries no UID.** It is `[TEXT]`, free text, not a requirement.
StrictDoc treats a requirement and free text as different things. You may mix them.

`samples/md-basic-en/` holds the same content written in `.md`. **Either form
produces the same traceability and the same JSON.** Only the way you write the body
text differs.

The files line up like this.

- `01-guide-for-human.sdoc` — this document. The reading order and how to write
- `02-upper.sdoc` — 3 system requirements. What to build
- `03-lower.sdoc` — 4 software requirements. How to build it. Links up to the system requirements
- `04-tests.sdoc` — 4 test cases. Links to the software requirements
- `05-review.sdoc` — 2 review findings. Links to the requirement each one targets
- `06-markdown.sdoc` — the document that writes its body text in Markdown notation
- `basic.sgra` — the grammar every document shares
- `strictdoc_config.py` — the project configuration. **StrictDoc reads it only from this folder**
- `_assets/` — figures, images and the `.md` files that links point to
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: The basics

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Text and tags

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
`.sdoc` has only three outward shapes.

- `[DOCUMENT]` — one per file. Put it first. Only `TITLE:` is mandatory
- `[GRAMMAR]` — the declaration of the fields you use. You can move it to a file
- a node — `[TEXT]` / `[[SECTION]]` / `[REQUIREMENT]`, plus the types the grammar adds

The smallest form is just this.

::

    [DOCUMENT]
    TITLE: Some specification

    [REQUIREMENT]
    UID: REQ-001
    TITLE: Doing something
    STATEMENT: >>>
    The system shall do something.
    <<<

**Fence a multi-line value with `>>>` and `<<<`.** Write a value that fits on one
line directly, as in `UID: REQ-001`. The way you write it, not the grammar
declaration, decides which form applies.

**Always give a node a `UID:`.** StrictDoc still parses a node without one, but
**nothing else can reference it and you cannot pull it out of the JSON.** Turn
anything you never need to pull — an explanation, a preface, the introduction to a
chapter — into `[TEXT]` and leave the UID off. Almost every node in this document
is `[TEXT]`.

**Move `[GRAMMAR]` into an external file so that every document matches.** In this
set five documents read the same `basic.sgra`.

::

    [GRAMMAR]
    IMPORT_FROM_FILE: basic.sgra

`basic.sgra` declares four node types.

- `SECTION` — a chapter. You can nest it
- `REQUIREMENT` — a requirement. `UID` / `STATUS` / `TITLE` / `STATEMENT` / `RATIONALE`
- `TEST_CASE` — a test case. It carries `EXPECTED`
- `FINDING` — a review finding. It carries `SEVERITY` and `RESOLUTION`

The last two are **not standard StrictDoc concepts.** The grammar adds them.
**Never add a field to a single document.** Every document then takes a different
shape, and nobody can tell where anything lives. Add the field to `basic.sgra`
instead.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: System requirements and software requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Write the link on the lower side.** Put `RELATIONS:` on the lower node and point
it at the UID of the parent. Write nothing on the upper side.

::

    RELATIONS:
    - TYPE: Parent
      VALUE: SYS-001

**The parent may live in another file.** StrictDoc collects every UID in the
project into one table and only then resolves the relations, so a file boundary
does not matter. This set works exactly that way.

::

    02-upper.sdoc   SYS-001   SYS-002   SYS-003
                       |         |         |
    03-lower.sdoc   SW-001    SW-002    SW-003 / SW-004
                       |         |         |
    04-tests.sdoc   TC-001    TC-002    TC-003 / TC-004

**Add `ROLE:` and the relation gains a meaning.** The type stays `Parent`.

::

    RELATIONS:
    - TYPE: Parent
      VALUE: SW-001
      ROLE: Verifies

`04-tests.sdoc` uses `Verifies` and `05-review.sdoc` uses `Reviews`. **Declare a
`ROLE` in the grammar before you use it.** StrictDoc fails on a value that nobody
declared.

Check the links on screen. Open `TRACEABILITY` or `DEEP TRACEABILITY` from
**VIEWS** above the document and the parents and children line up; open the
**traceability matrix** from the toolbar on the left and it lists every requirement
that no test covers.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Attachments

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Links

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
You can link to an `.md` file. This one points at `_assets/note.md`
→ [LINK: DOC-NOTE]

**The target only has to declare a UID right under its heading.**

::

    # Terminology map

    **UID**: DOC-NOTE

**Do not write the output path.** StrictDoc resolves it from the UID. StrictDoc
also builds the link text from the target's title, and **you cannot set that text
yourself.**

A `[LINK:]` may target a requirement too. You write `[LINK: SW-001]` the same way.

**StrictDoc parses every `.md` file in the project as a document, wherever it
sits.** `_assets/` is no exception. So always start an `.md` file with a `#`
heading. **One `.md` file without a heading stops the whole export.** Name the
files you truly cannot turn into documents in `exclude_doc_paths` in
`strictdoc_config.py` and leave them out.

**Exclude one file at a time. Never exclude a whole folder.**

::

    exclude_doc_paths=["_assets/notes.md"]   # OK. Only that one file drops out
    exclude_doc_paths=["_assets/**"]         # Wrong. The images vanish with it

The second form drops the folder from the scan, so **StrictDoc no longer treats it
as an asset folder and copies no images.** The export still reports success, and
only the images in the finished HTML return 404. You will hardly notice.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Figures

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
You can put a figure in by two routes.

**First — embed an image file.** Put it in `_assets/` and point at it with
`.. image::`. StrictDoc copies that folder on its own, so you add nothing to the
configuration. **Make SVG your default, because it survives any zoom.**

.. image:: _assets/flow.svg
   :alt: The flow from the input file to the output file, and the two stop conditions
   :width: 700px

**Second — move a Mermaid figure into an external file and include it.** The real
content lives in `_assets/fig-flow.sdoc`, and StrictDoc expands it right after this
paragraph.

::

    [DOCUMENT_FROM_FILE]
    FILE: _assets/fig-flow.sdoc

**Give the included file the extension `.sdoc`, not `.md`.**
`[DOCUMENT_FROM_FILE]` rejects anything else. You can still write the body in
Markdown — the fragment declares `MARKUP: Markdown` for itself.

::

    [DOCUMENT]
    TITLE: Figure - the conversion flow
    UID: FIG-FLOW
    OPTIONS:
      MARKUP: Markdown

    [TEXT]
    STATEMENT: >>>
    (write the Mermaid code fence here, as it is)
    <<<

**You cannot write this declaration inside `STATEMENT:`.** Put it in the body of a
document or a chapter. That is why the figure below sits outside this paragraph.

The included figure **appears directly on this screen and also opens as a document
of its own.** `strictdoc server` publishes an included document separately as well.
`export` does not publish it by default, so add ``--included-documents`` when you
need it. **Never add that option in order to query the JSON.** The same UID then
shows up in two places.

**An include is one to one.** You cannot include the same fragment in two
documents. To show it from two places, make one of them `[LINK: FIG-FLOW]`.
<<<

[DOCUMENT_FROM_FILE]
FILE: _assets/fig-flow.sdoc

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Tables

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
StrictDoc parses the body of an `.sdoc` file as RST by default. You can write a
table in two ways.

**First — the grid form.**

+----------+--------------------+-------------------------------------+
| Format   | If a column is off | Note                                |
+==========+====================+=====================================+
| grid     | export stops       | Align the cells in a monospace font |
+----------+--------------------+-------------------------------------+
| simple   | it survives        | Write the last rule for all columns |
+----------+--------------------+-------------------------------------+

**Second — the simple form.**

==========  ==================  ===================================
Format      If a column is off  Note
==========  ==================  ===================================
grid        export stops        Align the cells in a monospace font
simple      it survives         Write the last rule for all columns
==========  ==================  ===================================

**Both forms accept Japanese.** Count columns by **display width, not by character
count.** One full-width character takes 2 columns. **The table passes as long as it
lines up in a monospace font.**

**A grid table that is off by one column stops the whole export.** A simple table
still comes out correct when a cell runs to the right, so it is the safer choice
when you write by hand. **It does stop, though, if you leave the last rule short of
the full width.**

**A pipe table does not work by default.** It works only in a document that
declares `MARKUP: Markdown`. `06-markdown.sdoc` holds an example. **That form
passes without any column alignment.**

For a table with many columns you can also use `.. csv-table::`. That one passes
even when the column counts disagree.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Querying through JSON

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Export the whole specification to JSON, then pull just the part you want with
jq.** That is the whole of this chapter. Narrow the result **after** you export the
JSON.

**StrictDoc carries a query language of its own, but it does not reach the JSON.**
``--filter-nodes`` narrows only the `.sdoc` and the HTML output; on
``--formats=json`` it **emits every node and raises neither an error nor a
warning** (measured on 0.27.1). **So no road exists other than "export everything,
then query it."** Narrowing is jq's job.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Exporting the full JSON

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Three outputs exist. HTML is for a person to read, JSON is for a machine to
query, and the conversion to Markdown is for switching notation.**

::

    strictdoc export --formats=json --output-dir out .

StrictDoc writes the JSON to `out/json/index.json`. **Export it again every time
you edit a requirement.** ``--formats=json`` takes no narrowing option. **It always
emits everything.**

**You need no export just to read the HTML.** Drag this folder onto
`launch-strictdoc.bat` and the server starts; edit a file and save it, and the
screen picks the change up in about 0.6 seconds. The next chapter collects what you
need for switching notation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Querying with jq

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**jq is a small command that handles JSON and nothing else.** It has no connection
to StrictDoc, and `setup-strictdoc.bat` installs it by default. jq reads the JSON
and emits only what matches the condition you write.

::

    jq -r -f q-open-findings.jq out/json/index.json

To list the findings that nobody has resolved yet, `q-open-findings.jq` holds this.

::

    .DOCUMENTS[] | recurse(.NODES[]?)
    | select(._NODE_TYPE == "FINDING" and .RESOLUTION == "Open")
    | .UID + "  " + .SEVERITY + "  " + .TITLE

**The leading ``.`` means "the input itself."** From there ``.name`` descends and
``[]`` breaks an array apart. ``.DOCUMENTS[]`` means "each item of ``DOCUMENTS`` in
the input." The ``.`` is not an option but the filter itself, and it sits in the
middle of ``jq [options] <filter> [file]``.

**The key that gives you the node type is `_NODE_TYPE`.** It holds the name
`FINDING` that the grammar declares. The leading underscore makes it **easy to
mistype.** Run the filter against this set and it prints this.

::

    RV-001  Major  SW-002 does not say how to check the format
    RV-002  Question  SYS-003 offers no way to allow overwriting

**Run the same filter against the JSON from `samples/md-basic-en/` and not one
character of the output changes.** That is the proof of "either form produces the
same JSON."

**You write the filter in a file instead of passing it as a string because of
PowerShell.** PowerShell drops the double quotes inside a quoted string, which
breaks the form that writes the filter straight into the argument. ``-f`` behaves
the same in every shell.

**Type ``chcp 65001`` first when you pass Japanese through cmd.exe.** Left on the
default cp932, ``jq -r --arg kw 変換 -f ...`` returns **zero results and no
error.** PowerShell needs none of this.

`docs/03-sdoc-json-queries.md` holds 7 worked examples of queries.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Taking the answer as JSON

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Leave ``-r`` off and the answer comes out as JSON.** The example above added
``-r`` to build lines a person can read. Leave it off when you feed a program.

::

    jq -f q-findings-json.jq out/json/index.json

::

    [ .DOCUMENTS[] | recurse(.NODES[]?) | select(._NODE_TYPE == "FINDING") ]
    | map({UID, SEVERITY, RESOLUTION, TITLE})

::

    [
      {
        "UID": "RV-001",
        "SEVERITY": "Major",
        "RESOLUTION": "Open",
        "TITLE": "SW-002 does not say how to check the format"
      },
      {
        "UID": "RV-002",
        "SEVERITY": "Question",
        "RESOLUTION": "Open",
        "TITLE": "SYS-003 offers no way to allow overwriting"
      }
    ]

**The outer ``[ ... ]`` gathers the results, which arrive one at a time, into a
single array.** Without it jq only stacks JSON values one under another and builds
no array. ``map({UID, SEVERITY, RESOLUTION, TITLE})`` keeps just the fields you
want; drop that whole line when you want every field.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Non-ASCII characters

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Inside ``index.json`` StrictDoc turns every non-ASCII character into
``\uXXXX``.** A Japanese title reads like this in the file.

::

    "TITLE": "\u57fa\u672c - \u307e\u305a\u3053\u308c\u3092\u8aad\u3080",

StrictDoc calls ``json.dumps(..., indent=4)`` as it is, and Python escapes every
non-ASCII character by default. **StrictDoc offers no setting and no option that
changes this.**

**You never see this while you query with jq.** jq restores the original characters
as it reads, so every output example above comes out readable. **It matters only
when you hand the JSON file itself to a person or to a machine.** One pass through
jq fixes that case.

::

    jq . out/json/index.json > readable.json

**``.`` narrows nothing, so not one character of the content changes.** Only the
notation changes, **and the file gets smaller.**

=============  ======  =========
Output         Bytes   vs source
=============  ======  =========
index.json     92,021  3.51
after jq .     57,809  2.21
after jq -c .  36,756  1.40
=============  ======  =========

The ratios compare each file against the 26,212 bytes of the original `.sdoc` set.
A Japanese character such as ``検`` drops from 6 bytes back to the 3 bytes of
UTF-8, and the indent drops from 4 spaces to 2. ``-c`` strips the indent and the
newlines themselves.

**Never write this file out from PowerShell.** PowerShell's ``>`` writes UTF-16, so
you end up with a file that jq cannot read. Type the command in cmd.exe, or use the
form ``cmd /c 'jq . out/json/index.json > readable.json'``.

**Never explain JSON as "the lightweight option."** As the table above shows, even
the tightest form runs larger than the original `.sdoc`. The value lies not in the
size but in **pulling out the answer alone without reading the whole text.**
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Moving between .sdoc and .md

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
StrictDoc converts to and from `.md` in both directions.

::

    strictdoc export --formats=sdoc     --output-dir out .   # .md -> .sdoc
    strictdoc export --formats=markdown --output-dir out .   # .sdoc -> .md

**`strictdoc convert` handles Excel and ReqIF only, and it does not do this.**

**Create the document as `.md` and you get Markdown notation from the browser
editor alone.** No way exists to switch a document you created as `.sdoc` over to
Markdown from the browser, so this is one practical reason to choose `.md`.
`samples/md-basic-en/02-guide-for-human.md` describes the `.md` set and what to
watch for during a conversion.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
